@Pragma @Lucien @Rifzio Doge di Venezia : "It's a tricky question, partially contributed to by ambiguous uses of the term 'materialism' in philosophy. Philosophers who propose that materialism is identical to physicalism (the doctrine that everything is physical) generally find little to agree with in Nietzsche after confronting his science scepticism. These would be the 'materialists' whom Nietzsche criticises, guided by his attacks on Darwinism.

However, other definitions of materialism are more amenable to accepting Nietzsche into their fold. I've seen Nietzsche characterised as a 'vitalist materialist' as well, alongside Bergson and Deleuze. This works as an identification because of his use of the Will to Power - he treats it as the substance of which everything is made (in his later works at least), and thus as the 'matter' of his theory. However, Nietzsche cannot be a physicalist because of his faith in vitalism and irrationalism (which are at odds with the scientific bent of modern physicalism).

For vitalist materialists, everything is made of some 'living force' (elán vital), such as the Will to Power in Nietzsche, the Preindividual in Simondon, and Difference in Deleuze. They can still be intelligibly called 'materialists' (rather than idealists) since they prioritise Being over Thinking, by giving Being the name of the elán vital.

